WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.237 --> 00:00:02.247
- Andrew, wanna get some ribs?

00:00:02.247 --> 00:00:03.080
- Yes.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:03.913
- Hey, that was easy.

00:00:03.913 --> 00:00:04.746
Alright, let's get some ribs.

00:00:04.746 --> 00:00:07.191
Oh wait, tomorrow, I'm busy today, sorry.

00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:08.122
- Ah (beep).

00:00:08.122 --> 00:00:11.025
- American barbecue,
arguably the best barbecue.

00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.696
Brisket, pork shoulder.

00:00:12.696 --> 00:00:13.847
- There's lots of cuts of meat.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.158
We're focusing on ribs.

00:00:15.158 --> 00:00:16.633
Today on Worth It,

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.434
we're going to be trying
3 barbecue rib spots.

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:21.060
At three drastically
different price points

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.522
to find out which one is the
most worth it, at its price.

00:00:24.522 --> 00:00:25.355
- Worth it.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:26.745
- We must be caveat at this with the fact

00:00:26.745 --> 00:00:28.761
that we are doing this in Los Angeles.

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:30.423
- Los Angeles has some very good barbecue.

00:00:30.423 --> 00:00:32.232
- It does, it's not the South.

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:35.175
Turns out, there's a lot of
great barbecue, in Long Beach?

00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:36.812
- And if you don't know
where Long Beach is,

00:00:36.812 --> 00:00:39.010
look on a map (chuckles).

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.427
(jazz music)

00:00:42.349 --> 00:00:43.495
- [Neil] Hey guys, my
name is Neil Strawder,

00:00:43.495 --> 00:00:44.682
also known as BigMista,

00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:46.289
of BigMista's Barbecue and Sammich Shop.

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:48.642
Barbecue is originally about
taking a bad piece of meat

00:00:48.642 --> 00:00:50.585
and making something wonderful out of it.

00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:51.876
We're doing better quality meat

00:00:51.876 --> 00:00:52.991
and creating something amazing.

00:00:52.991 --> 00:00:55.771
- How would you define your
particular brand of barbecue.

00:00:55.771 --> 00:00:57.365
- People assume that
because I'm from Texas,

00:00:57.365 --> 00:00:58.933
I cook Texas style barbecue.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:00.777
There is some Texas
influence but I've learned

00:01:00.777 --> 00:01:02.237
from people all over the country.

00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:03.170
It's BigMista style.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.942
I take whole spare ribs,
trim them, add our rub on it,

00:01:05.942 --> 00:01:07.795
which is called BigMista's
Perfect Pork Rub.

00:01:07.795 --> 00:01:08.773
- [Steven] Is that a secret rub?

00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:11.794
- Yes, you wanna know what the secret is?

00:01:11.794 --> 00:01:12.627
Salt.

00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:14.157
- (all laughing) Salt.

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:15.670
- And it goes in the smoker for

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.857
about three and a half, four hours.

00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:17.953
- [Andrew] And what kinda smoke

00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:19.087
are you putting in those ribs?

00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:21.038
- What we cook on it's
called a pellet smoker,

00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:22.710
it holds 240 pounds of meat.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.140
We're using pecan and cherry,
smoke is an ingredient,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.086
you know, it's like paprika or salt.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.795
It should blend with
the other ingredients.

00:01:28.795 --> 00:01:29.831
It's temperature goes down,

00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:31.103
it'll shoot some of
these pellets down into

00:01:31.103 --> 00:01:32.596
what's called a little fire pod.

00:01:32.596 --> 00:01:34.415
And there's a convection
fan that blows the smoke

00:01:34.415 --> 00:01:36.219
and the heat down across the meat.

00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:37.756
- More traditional barbecuers,

00:01:37.756 --> 00:01:39.381
do they shy away from this technology?

00:01:39.381 --> 00:01:42.053
- It used to be considered
cooking on the dark side.

00:01:42.053 --> 00:01:43.554
You know, you have to cut logs

00:01:43.554 --> 00:01:44.968
and burn them down into coals.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:45.801
I don't know about y'all,

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:47.038
but I don't wanna do all
that work, I just wanna eat.

00:01:47.038 --> 00:01:49.737
We have regular sauce, then
we have the spicy sauce.

00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:51.644
It'll make your lips tingle but
it won't blow your head off.

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:52.477
That cool with you?

00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:54.856
- Yeah, I can do spicy 'cause later.

00:01:54.856 --> 00:01:55.836
- The spicy does you.

00:01:55.836 --> 00:01:56.669
- Yeah.

00:01:56.669 --> 00:01:57.843
(Andrew laughs loudly)

00:01:57.843 --> 00:01:58.713
- See this off on the side?

00:01:58.713 --> 00:02:00.035
Dip it, pour it on top.

00:02:00.035 --> 00:02:00.868
What do you think I should do here?

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:01.936
- It's barbecue, just eat it.

00:02:01.936 --> 00:02:02.769
- Okay, got it.

00:02:02.769 --> 00:02:05.735
- [Andrew] My old friend, Shasta.

00:02:05.735 --> 00:02:06.765
- Cheers.

00:02:06.765 --> 00:02:07.598
That's not bad.

00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:08.431
- I like cream soda.

00:02:08.431 --> 00:02:10.022
That was always my favorite as a kid.

00:02:10.022 --> 00:02:11.865
This rib looks slightly
bigger so I'm gonna take it.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:14.222
Cheers. (Steven clicks his tongue)

00:02:14.222 --> 00:02:15.826
(jazz music)

00:02:15.826 --> 00:02:19.045
- Wow, clean off the bone
but still holding together.

00:02:19.045 --> 00:02:22.121
I love the rub, it has
a very satisfying crust

00:02:22.121 --> 00:02:23.778
and it's a little bit spicy.

00:02:23.778 --> 00:02:27.115
- When I think barbecue, I
think ribs, drooling with sauce.

00:02:27.115 --> 00:02:30.950
When you have great smoke,
this is perfect as it is.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.742
- I've always thought it was
kinda like nonsense the way

00:02:32.742 --> 00:02:34.382
that dogs chew on bones.

00:02:34.382 --> 00:02:35.330
Like what are they doing?

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.506
Like why is that fun for you?

00:02:36.506 --> 00:02:39.239
But for the first time
in my life I'm like.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:41.037
I kinda know what you mean guys.

00:02:41.037 --> 00:02:42.017
(Steven laughs)

00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:44.427
- Rib break here for the beans.

00:02:44.427 --> 00:02:45.415
Oh my god, this is mine.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:46.248
- Get off of me.

00:02:46.248 --> 00:02:47.254
- This is all mine.

00:02:47.254 --> 00:02:48.416
There's a lot of sugar in here.

00:02:48.416 --> 00:02:50.056
- There's also a lot of pork in here.

00:02:50.056 --> 00:02:51.352
This is like a stew.

00:02:51.352 --> 00:02:53.984
You wanna just break that
in half, break bread.

00:02:53.984 --> 00:02:55.201
(gentle rock music)

00:02:55.201 --> 00:02:57.244
Barbecue is the best food, yes.

00:02:57.244 --> 00:02:59.248
- I knew you were gonna say
that, jinx, double jinx.

00:02:59.248 --> 00:03:01.236
You can't talk anymore,
I get to eat everything.

00:03:01.236 --> 00:03:02.601
I'm going spicy sauce.

00:03:02.601 --> 00:03:03.671
You see that? Oh.

00:03:03.671 --> 00:03:05.004
Cheers.

00:03:05.004 --> 00:03:08.213
(jazz music)

00:03:08.213 --> 00:03:09.911
You're gonna need to give me a moment.

00:03:09.911 --> 00:03:11.920
I'm gonna, I gotta eat this this way.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.816
- Just censor Steven's face.

00:03:14.816 --> 00:03:15.649
- Woo!

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:19.655
(Steven coughs)

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.770
- Yes die, leave the food for me.

00:03:24.943 --> 00:03:25.776
Oh!

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:27.918
That was the winner.

00:03:27.918 --> 00:03:29.398
Stop watching the episode now.

00:03:29.398 --> 00:03:30.918
- BigMista threw us a bone.

00:03:30.918 --> 00:03:32.696
- In the middle of shooting he revealed

00:03:32.696 --> 00:03:35.925
that he had planned a BBQ fact for us.

00:03:35.925 --> 00:03:37.170
This is what he said.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.492
- Now I've seen you guys
do your thing before.

00:03:39.492 --> 00:03:41.470
I want to jump in with this.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.327
Rib Fact!

00:03:42.327 --> 00:03:43.632
(all laughing)

00:03:43.632 --> 00:03:44.590
- Alright.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.102
- They're generally four types of ribs

00:03:46.102 --> 00:03:47.166
that you see in-store.

00:03:47.166 --> 00:03:49.430
You're regular spare ribs,
the St. Louis cut rib,

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:51.216
your baby backs, and
your country-style ribs.

00:03:51.216 --> 00:03:53.785
First thing, country-style
ribs are not ribs.

00:03:53.785 --> 00:03:55.991
They actually come from the pork butt.

00:03:55.991 --> 00:03:56.824
Bonus rib fact!

00:03:56.824 --> 00:03:58.790
The pork butt is actually
not the pork butt.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:03:59.860
It is not from the pig's butt,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.005
it's actually from the shoulder.

00:04:01.005 --> 00:04:01.838
- I didn't not know
that did you know that?

00:04:01.838 --> 00:04:02.992
- The pig is a confusing animal.

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:04.591
It's magical but confusing.

00:04:04.591 --> 00:04:08.392
- Let's just react to that fact now.

00:04:08.392 --> 00:04:09.837
Wow, so those are the (laughs)

00:04:09.837 --> 00:04:11.002
(both laughing)

00:04:11.002 --> 00:04:13.919
(smooth music)

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:15.615
- My name is Rory Herrmann
I'm the executive chef,

00:04:15.615 --> 00:04:17.122
here at Barrel and Ashes.

00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:19.300
I think the most important
thing about barbecue

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.190
and you have to remember
this is it's slow and low.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.149
We start our fire at about
three in the morning.

00:04:23.149 --> 00:04:25.588
And we'll let that burn
'til about 4:00 or 4:30

00:04:25.588 --> 00:04:27.289
before the ribs will even go in there.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:28.404
- I'm jealous of your neighbors,

00:04:28.404 --> 00:04:30.628
they must be asleep at
night somewhere around four

00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:33.128
in the morning they start
smelling barbecue smoke.

00:04:33.128 --> 00:04:34.686
- Today we're using a Duroc rib,

00:04:34.686 --> 00:04:36.261
it's a little bit darker of a meat.

00:04:36.261 --> 00:04:37.564
Has a little bit more blood in it.

00:04:37.564 --> 00:04:39.177
That way if we cook it slow and low,

00:04:39.177 --> 00:04:40.566
it contains a lot of moisture.

00:04:40.566 --> 00:04:42.768
And we season it very
simply with just Kosher salt

00:04:42.768 --> 00:04:44.963
and Tellicherry black
pepper which we hand grind.

00:04:44.963 --> 00:04:47.185
We really want it to taste
like the great meat that it is

00:04:47.185 --> 00:04:48.231
and the smoke that it is.

00:04:48.231 --> 00:04:49.704
We use Red Oak specifically

00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:51.312
because it's a little
bit denser of a wood.

00:04:51.312 --> 00:04:53.385
And we'll cook our ribs
for about five hours

00:04:53.385 --> 00:04:54.622
at two hundred and twenty degrees.

00:04:54.622 --> 00:04:56.199
After that cooking process,

00:04:56.199 --> 00:04:58.077
we use a process called crutching it.

00:04:58.077 --> 00:05:00.411
We'll wrap and allow them
to rest for about an hour.

00:05:00.411 --> 00:05:01.922
What that does is allow the moisture

00:05:01.922 --> 00:05:03.110
to come back into the rib,

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.946
the juices and flavor within it.

00:05:04.946 --> 00:05:06.090
And then we have a mop sauce.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.484
It's what we actually brush the ribs

00:05:07.484 --> 00:05:08.897
with right before we
touch them on the grill.

00:05:08.897 --> 00:05:10.885
Which consists of Arkansas
city barbecue sauce,

00:05:10.885 --> 00:05:12.577
molasses, some chili flakes,

00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:14.303
a little bit of mustard
and red wine vinegar.

00:05:14.303 --> 00:05:15.577
Just to touch on that
grill and get a little bit

00:05:15.577 --> 00:05:16.998
of tackiness and a little bite to it.

00:05:16.998 --> 00:05:18.576
- So how much is the
full rack of ribs here?

00:05:18.576 --> 00:05:21.136
- 36 dollars, half rack is 19.

00:05:21.136 --> 00:05:22.925
Almost everybody that orders the half rack

00:05:22.925 --> 00:05:24.733
turns around and goes we're
gonna need another half.

00:05:24.733 --> 00:05:25.566
(all laughing)

00:05:25.566 --> 00:05:26.727
Might as well just order the
whole rack to begin with.

00:05:26.727 --> 00:05:27.592
- [Steven] Tell me about your drink.

00:05:27.592 --> 00:05:29.806
- It's a barrel aged bourbon drink.

00:05:29.806 --> 00:05:32.085
You really effed up by
always being the driver.

00:05:32.085 --> 00:05:33.696
- Somebody's gotta DD. (glass clanging)

00:05:33.696 --> 00:05:34.529
Cheers.

00:05:34.529 --> 00:05:36.513
- That was so very aggressive.

00:05:36.513 --> 00:05:37.820
Shall I carve you a rib?

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:38.653
- Please.

00:05:38.653 --> 00:05:39.857
- This is the first time we've had ribs

00:05:39.857 --> 00:05:42.995
that are dressed up with a little sauce.

00:05:42.995 --> 00:05:43.828
- Cheers.

00:05:44.778 --> 00:05:45.611
- Mm.

00:05:47.531 --> 00:05:48.489
- That's my winner.

00:05:48.489 --> 00:05:51.065
- Oh, this is a much more
a really seasoned rib.

00:05:51.065 --> 00:05:52.470
And I like that a lot.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.502
- So the slow cooking here,

00:05:53.502 --> 00:05:55.430
it's a lot longer than BigMista's.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.191
It definitely pays off,
the meat is really loose.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.285
- It's more aggressive smoke flavor.

00:06:00.285 --> 00:06:01.683
Personally I kind of like that.

00:06:01.683 --> 00:06:03.830
- One of our most famous
sides is the Hoe Cake.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.197
It's our version of corn
bread made to order.

00:06:06.197 --> 00:06:08.935
Take a hot cast iron pan and
we pour that batter into it.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.983
Into the wood oven and we hit
it with maple honey butter,

00:06:10.983 --> 00:06:12.846
malt and salt, and green onions,

00:06:12.846 --> 00:06:13.937
a little cracked black pepper.

00:06:13.937 --> 00:06:15.566
Comes sizzling at the
table and let me tell ya,

00:06:15.566 --> 00:06:16.524
you can't miss that with barbecue.

00:06:16.524 --> 00:06:17.357
- [Andrew] That's the one, yeah.

00:06:17.357 --> 00:06:18.299
- This kinda looks like flan.

00:06:18.299 --> 00:06:21.175
- Yeah, it has like a
really nice soft texture.

00:06:21.175 --> 00:06:23.248
(cello music)

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:24.081
- Oh.

00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:27.123
- This is really good.

00:06:27.123 --> 00:06:28.980
It's really like a creme brulee cornbread.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.120
- Holy crap and the scallions on top.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.102
In China's Cuisine,
there's a scallion pancake.

00:06:33.102 --> 00:06:33.935
- Yes.

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:35.148
- This is like that but..

00:06:35.148 --> 00:06:35.981
- Cornbread, yeah.

00:06:35.981 --> 00:06:36.814
- Dessert version of it.

00:06:36.814 --> 00:06:40.981
I think Adam should try this
right now while it's hot.

00:06:42.024 --> 00:06:43.220
(Andrew and Steven laugh)

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.053
- [Andrew] Rib tips.

00:06:44.053 --> 00:06:44.886
- Ooh!

00:06:46.674 --> 00:06:47.507
Oh!

00:06:51.434 --> 00:06:52.658
(Steven groans)

00:06:52.658 --> 00:06:53.495
That's like a delicacy.

00:06:53.495 --> 00:06:54.328
- Oh god.

00:06:54.328 --> 00:06:59.219
I'm having a full body
experience with this pork.

00:06:59.219 --> 00:07:01.376
- This is making me uncomfortable.

00:07:01.376 --> 00:07:02.209
Okay.

00:07:02.209 --> 00:07:04.112
- The next location of
barbecue is gonna have

00:07:04.112 --> 00:07:06.213
to wait until another day. (belches)

00:07:06.213 --> 00:07:07.691
Nice.

00:07:07.691 --> 00:07:08.796
- Not nice.

00:07:08.796 --> 00:07:09.770
(Andrew groans)

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.128
- Again, I'm feeling
a little uncomfortable

00:07:11.128 --> 00:07:14.422
with you making noise, closing your eyes.

00:07:14.422 --> 00:07:15.931
I can't be here now, anymore.

00:07:15.931 --> 00:07:16.931
That's how you know it's good.

00:07:16.931 --> 00:07:18.601
- What do you think human ribs taste like?

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:20.033
- Depends on the human.

00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:22.390
What do you think human
anything taste like?

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:23.422
- BBQ Fact!

00:07:23.422 --> 00:07:25.140
- What do you think the longest barbecue

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.068
in terms of time wise for
a single person barbecuing.

00:07:29.068 --> 00:07:30.029
- 96 hours.

00:07:30.029 --> 00:07:32.404
- The answer is 80 hours.

00:07:32.404 --> 00:07:33.697
- Oh, I was close.

00:07:33.697 --> 00:07:34.571
- You were close.

00:07:34.571 --> 00:07:36.764
He cooked 1,000 hot
dogs, 200 pieces of corn,

00:07:36.764 --> 00:07:38.785
a hundred and four pieces of chicken,

00:07:38.785 --> 00:07:41.941
500 burgers and 526 sausages.

00:07:41.941 --> 00:07:43.069
- How many ribs do humans have?

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:46.630
- In a rack of pork ribs,
you get about 14 I believe.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.226
(soft scratching)

00:07:48.226 --> 00:07:50.366
Do all mammals have the
same number of ribs?

00:07:50.366 --> 00:07:51.348
- I counted 11.

00:07:51.348 --> 00:07:52.181
(ding)

00:07:52.181 --> 00:07:53.734
- You know how a giraffe
has the same number

00:07:53.734 --> 00:07:55.965
of vertebrae as a human, in their neck.

00:07:55.965 --> 00:07:56.798
- No.

00:07:56.798 --> 00:07:57.908
- That's a fact.

00:07:57.908 --> 00:07:59.176
- How's that possible?

00:07:59.176 --> 00:08:00.408
- They're just really big.

00:08:00.408 --> 00:08:03.372
(piano music)

00:08:03.372 --> 00:08:04.791
- Hey, I'm Daniel Weinstock.

00:08:04.791 --> 00:08:06.165
That's my partner, Mike Garrett.

00:08:06.165 --> 00:08:08.223
We're the owners of Maple Block Meat Co.

00:08:08.223 --> 00:08:11.821
Today we have a full rack
of wood smoked spare ribs.

00:08:11.821 --> 00:08:14.864
- We've always had a passion
and love for barbecue.

00:08:14.864 --> 00:08:16.800
We had been influenced and
introduced to some stuff

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.347
in Texas and we're just
blown away by their process

00:08:19.347 --> 00:08:20.866
and their attention to detail.

00:08:20.866 --> 00:08:22.727
We believed if could bring
anything close to that,

00:08:22.727 --> 00:08:25.106
here to Los Angeles,
we'd be great success.

00:08:25.106 --> 00:08:27.070
- [Daniel] We get them
in, we inspect them first.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.861
We got an eye for the general
shape and size and marbling.

00:08:29.861 --> 00:08:31.621
Trim those, our rubs are simple,

00:08:31.621 --> 00:08:33.565
a few ingredients with the right ratios.

00:08:33.565 --> 00:08:35.840
They'll smoke for about
four hours with peach wood.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.479
Every barbecue culture uses a wood

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:38.974
that's indigenous to their areas.

00:08:38.974 --> 00:08:41.902
Burns nice and clean, gets nice and sweet.

00:08:41.902 --> 00:08:43.679
- [Mike] And it's unique to
us, I don't think anybody else

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.089
use peach wood as their primary wood.

00:08:46.089 --> 00:08:47.964
So it's all a commitment to the flavor.

00:08:47.964 --> 00:08:48.999
- [Daniel] Come out beautiful,

00:08:48.999 --> 00:08:50.709
given the right amount of time to rest.

00:08:50.709 --> 00:08:51.715
- So your Harvey station is here?

00:08:51.715 --> 00:08:54.215
- We've always wanted this
to be kind of the meat alter.

00:08:54.215 --> 00:08:56.325
As you walk in the door,
it's the first thing you see.

00:08:56.325 --> 00:08:58.451
It's got almost the sushi bar effect.

00:08:58.451 --> 00:09:00.347
- Is it hard running
a barbecue restaurant?

00:09:00.347 --> 00:09:02.461
The temptation everyday, just.

00:09:02.461 --> 00:09:03.759
- We do the quality check, you know,

00:09:03.759 --> 00:09:06.521
costs a lot but we're
dedicated meat heads for sure.

00:09:06.521 --> 00:09:07.563
- We'd help you out by the way.

00:09:07.563 --> 00:09:09.103
Give us a call if you need some help.

00:09:09.103 --> 00:09:11.015
- [Mike] Designated eaters, yeah.

00:09:11.015 --> 00:09:14.252
- This a hand made strawberry
soda and a classic beer

00:09:14.252 --> 00:09:16.091
for all barbecue with Shiner Bock.

00:09:16.091 --> 00:09:17.049
- Thank you very much.

00:09:17.049 --> 00:09:20.089
- To barbe-me into barbecue.

00:09:20.089 --> 00:09:23.362
I love pickles because they
are the perfect little snack.

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.806
It's a food and a drink.

00:09:25.806 --> 00:09:28.110
I like that the rib tip is still attached.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.387
That fatty, chewy, morsal,
dangling off the end.

00:09:31.387 --> 00:09:32.883
- Let's cross swords.

00:09:32.883 --> 00:09:34.482
(metallic cling)

00:09:34.482 --> 00:09:36.982
(piano music)

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:39.644
Holy crap.

00:09:39.644 --> 00:09:41.680
- That is one of the nicest smoke tastes.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.017
It's so light, you're at summer camp

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:45.806
and you're heading to
the nightly camp fire

00:09:45.806 --> 00:09:47.143
but it's still pretty far away.

00:09:47.143 --> 00:09:48.903
But you catch the first whiffs of it.

00:09:48.903 --> 00:09:50.079
- Yeah, hit me with some sauces.

00:09:50.079 --> 00:09:51.347
- Here you go, you want some more?

00:09:51.347 --> 00:09:52.202
- Yes please.

00:09:52.202 --> 00:09:54.632
Ribs, we've learned, not a clean snack.

00:09:54.632 --> 00:09:56.028
(piano music)

00:09:56.028 --> 00:09:57.085
("Mm" in unison)

00:09:57.085 --> 00:10:00.291
- Gently sweet, it's got like
this molasses sweetness to it.

00:10:00.291 --> 00:10:03.578
- Everything is tender and
gentle and feels like...

00:10:03.578 --> 00:10:04.411
- Subtle.

00:10:04.411 --> 00:10:05.244
- Being wooed.

00:10:05.244 --> 00:10:07.785
You wanna try some sides?

00:10:07.785 --> 00:10:09.102
- Are you gonna wait for me?

00:10:09.102 --> 00:10:09.935
- Yeah.

00:10:09.935 --> 00:10:12.352
(soft piano)

00:10:14.545 --> 00:10:16.796
This food gets me, it
knows what I wanna eat.

00:10:16.796 --> 00:10:18.724
- Yeah because it's good food.

00:10:18.724 --> 00:10:20.263
This food gets me too.

00:10:20.263 --> 00:10:21.274
- I get you.

00:10:21.274 --> 00:10:22.107
- No you don't.

00:10:22.107 --> 00:10:22.940
Let's try a biscuit.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:24.234
Do you wanna share half of one?

00:10:24.234 --> 00:10:25.720
(Steven grumbles)

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.439
I wanna save a little biscuit for Adam.

00:10:27.439 --> 00:10:29.599
(exhales) it's all about
that biscuit smell.

00:10:29.599 --> 00:10:31.890
- Alright, let's cheers it. Ready?

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.752
- I'm not done buttering my biscuit.

00:10:33.752 --> 00:10:36.169
(soft piano)

00:10:37.247 --> 00:10:38.386
Holy shi- (beep).

00:10:38.386 --> 00:10:41.254
- (laughs) I was just
about to say, oh my god.

00:10:41.254 --> 00:10:45.161
You have to try this right now, Adam.

00:10:45.161 --> 00:10:46.730
This is insane.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:47.563
- Yeah?

00:10:47.563 --> 00:10:50.184
- That's the best biscuit I've ever had.

00:10:50.184 --> 00:10:51.267
What? No!

00:10:51.267 --> 00:10:52.100
Not yet!

00:10:54.633 --> 00:10:56.513
- How is that?

00:10:56.513 --> 00:10:58.483
Steven stop doing that.

00:10:58.483 --> 00:10:59.316
- It's a bird.

00:10:59.316 --> 00:11:01.415
- There's a bird above us and
it might take a sh- (beep).

00:11:01.415 --> 00:11:02.248
- Oh.

00:11:02.248 --> 00:11:03.081
- Okay, it's gone.

00:11:03.081 --> 00:11:04.842
- We're gonna go with just the ribs.

00:11:04.842 --> 00:11:06.525
So based on the ribs alone,

00:11:06.525 --> 00:11:09.272
which barbecue spot was the
most worth it at its price.

00:11:09.272 --> 00:11:11.039
- Barrel and Ashes probably had

00:11:11.039 --> 00:11:12.772
the biggest flavor of the day.

00:11:12.772 --> 00:11:16.745
And they were kinda the perfect
TV ad of a barbecue rib.

00:11:16.745 --> 00:11:17.963
- This was the worst idea.

00:11:17.963 --> 00:11:18.796
- What?

00:11:18.796 --> 00:11:20.445
- Eating popsicles while doing a wrap up

00:11:20.445 --> 00:11:22.823
'cause your literally at
the mercy of the melting.

00:11:22.823 --> 00:11:23.826
This is his idea by the way.

00:11:23.826 --> 00:11:26.203
- But my favorite and
my worth it winner was

00:11:26.203 --> 00:11:28.850
Neil's from BigMista's Sammich Shop.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:30.767
They were perfectly balanced

00:11:30.767 --> 00:11:32.913
and it was a really powerful rub.

00:11:32.913 --> 00:11:34.066
They were just perfect to me.

00:11:34.066 --> 00:11:36.973
- The smokey flavor of Maple
Block was probably my favorite

00:11:36.973 --> 00:11:39.828
but my worth it winner
goes to Barrel and Ashes.

00:11:39.828 --> 00:11:41.788
It's reasonably priced,
perfect for a date,

00:11:41.788 --> 00:11:44.553
perfect for a family outing,
perfect to not break your bank.

00:11:44.553 --> 00:11:45.762
And those rib tips.

00:11:45.762 --> 00:11:48.350
- Adam what is your winner?

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:52.604
Nothing compliments
delicious meat on a bone

00:11:52.604 --> 00:11:55.483
than delicious frozen desert on a stick.

00:11:55.483 --> 00:11:57.165
And that's why we're eating popsicles.

00:11:57.165 --> 00:11:58.314
- Mine kinda looks like a turd now.

00:11:58.314 --> 00:11:59.663
- Way to...

00:11:59.663 --> 00:12:00.797
Bring it all back down.

00:12:00.797 --> 00:12:02.426
- I'mma finish mine, here we go.

00:12:02.426 --> 00:12:03.936
(slurps) Oh.

00:12:03.936 --> 00:12:04.952
- [Andrew] Brain freeze?

00:12:04.952 --> 00:12:07.564
I know your weakness Steven.

00:12:07.564 --> 00:12:08.397
It's the brain freeze.

00:12:08.397 --> 00:12:11.814
- It's not a weakness, it's just so cold.

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:13.498
Worth it.

00:12:13.498 --> 00:12:15.081
- [Andrew] Oh yes!

